{"id":"c9548800-3fd1-41dd-990e-fa3427c5a037","object":"chat.completion","created":1784837551,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n    removable = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != 'root']\\n    removable.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    def retained_descendants(node_id):\\n        desc = set()\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur not in retained:\\n                continue\\n            desc.add(cur)\\n            for child in children[cur]:\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return desc\\n    for node in removable:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        subtree = retained_descendants(nid)\\n        if not subtree:\\n            continue\\n        symbols_after = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid not in subtree:\\n                symbols_after.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\\n            for rid in subtree:\\n                retained.discard(rid)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained': sorted_retained, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is Python source code for solve(case). The task is about grammar-guided node reduction, similar to Perses. The function receives a case with nodes and required_symbols. We need to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes that are not required, while preserving that required symbols are present, and that the tree remains \"grammar valid\" and \"interestingness preserved\". However, the problem description mentions \"bounded objective\" and \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar\". But we don't have an actual grammar or interestingness oracle. The fixtures only provide nodes and required_symbols. So we need to implement a reduction algorithm that respects the rules: do not remove root, required symbols, nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove nodes only if all required symbols remain represented (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols present (from retained nodes) must still include required_symbols). Also consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes. Use node ID as tie-breaker at equal depth.\n\nWe need to return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order. The output format: return a JSON-serializable object, likely a dict with \"retained\" and \"removal_order\". Or maybe just a list? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we can return a dict: {\"retained\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}.\n\nThe algorithm: Start with all nodes. Identify removable nodes (removable=true and not root). Process nodes in order: first by depth descending (deeper first), then by node ID (string? likely alphabetical). For each removable node, tentatively simulate removal: check that after removal, all required symbols are still present among remaining nodes. Also, the node must have no children that are retained (i.e., if we remove it, its children would become orphaned? Actually, the tree structure: parent-child relationships. If we remove a node, we need to decide what happens to its children. In Perses, removal of a node means its children become children of its parent, but that might violate grammar. However, the problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But we don't have grammar. The rule \"Never remove a node with retained children\" suggests we can only remove a node if it has no children that are currently retained? Or if we remove a node, its children are also removed? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies removal of the node itself, but children? Possibly the node's children would be removed as well if the node is removed? In tree reduction, typically removing a node means removing the entire subtree rooted at that node, unless we have replacement. But the phrase \"replacement candidates\" suggests we might replace a node with one of its children or something. However, the fixture only has \"removable\" boolean, not replacement info. So we likely just either keep or remove nodes entirely. The rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" implies that if a node has children that are to be retained (not removed), we cannot remove the parent. But if we remove the parent, its children would be gone? Or they would be reassigned? The problem is ambiguous. Given the context of Perses, reduction removes subtrees. Usually, if you remove a node, all its descendants are removed. So removing a node implicitly removes its children. Therefore, we cannot remove a node if any of its descendants are required or need to be kept for interestingness. However, the rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that we check if the node has any child that is currently retained (i.e., not removed). If it does, we cannot remove the node. But if we plan to remove the node, its children would also be removed, so they wouldn't be retained. So the condition is: before removal, the node's children (and their subtrees) must not contain any node that we intend to keep. Since we are iteratively removing, we need to keep track of which nodes are still in the tree (retained). So we can start with all nodes retained. Then process removable nodes in order, and for each, if it has any retained descendants (i.e., any node in its subtree that is still retained), skip removal. Otherwise, check that after removal, the required symbols are still present in the set of retained nodes (excluding this node and its subtree). If yes, then remove the node and its entire subtree. Record the removal order.\n\nBut we also need to consider that some nodes may be removable but have children that are removable and could be removed first? Since we process deeper nodes first, we will remove leaf nodes first, then possibly their parents. This aligns with typical reduction: try to remove deeper nodes before shallower ones. So algorithm: Build a tree structure from nodes using parent references. For each node, store its children list. Also map id to node. Also compute depth (given). Then sort removable nodes by depth descending, then by id (string compare? Or numeric? The ids are like \"n1\",\"n6\" etc. They are strings. Use string comparison as tie-breaker. Then for each node in that order:\n\n1. If the node is not currently retained (i.e., already removed by a parent removal), skip.\n2. If the node has any retained children (i.e., any child that is still in the retained set), skip. Because we cannot remove a node with retained children.\n3. Tentatively remove the node and its entire subtree (all descendants). Check if the set of symbols from all still-retained nodes (excluding this subtree) contains all required_symbols. Required_symbols include \"root\" which is always present. But root is not removable. So after removal, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. Note: root's symbol is \"root\". That's always required. Others: \"s1\" etc.\n4. If condition holds, then actually remove: mark all nodes in the subtree as removed. Append the node's id to removal_order.\n5. Continue.\n\nFinally, collect all nodes that are still retained (including root and non-removable nodes that were not removed). Sort their ids (alphabetical? The problem says \"sorted retained IDs\". Probably alphabetical or whatever natural order. Usually ID is string; sort alphabetically). Return a dict with \"retained\" list and \"removal_order\" list.\n\nWe need to be careful: The rule \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we should never attempt to remove root (removable is false anyway). Also required symbols: do we consider a node that has a required symbol but the node itself is removable? In the fixtures, some nodes have symbol \"s1\" and are removable, but \"s1\" is a required symbol. So we cannot remove a node that is the only one providing a required symbol. The condition about required symbols being represented after removal handles that. But also the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That might mean we should not remove a node that has a required symbol, regardless of whether it is the only one? But the condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" implies that if there are multiple nodes with the same required symbol, we could remove one as long as another remains. So the rule \"Never remove a required symbol\" might be interpreted as never remove a node that is the only representative of that required symbol? But the text says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That seems absolute: if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we should not remove it. But then the \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be moot because you'd never remove a node that has a required symbol. Let's examine fixture: In case a-000, nodes: root (root, not removable), n1 (s1, removable=false so cannot remove anyway), n2 (s2, removable=true, but s2 is not required), n4 (s1, removable=true, and s1 is required). So n4 is removable but has required symbol s1. If we interpret \"Never remove a required symbol\" literally, we cannot remove n4. But then the algorithm would skip it. However, the problem might mean \"never remove a node whose symbol is in the required set\"? That would be too restrictive. More likely, \"required symbol\" refers to the root and any symbols that are mandatory for the parse tree to be valid? In Perses, you cannot remove a node that would cause the grammar to be violated, but the required symbols are the ones that must be present for interestingness. The interestingness oracle may require certain symbols. So the rule is that after removal, all required symbols must still be present. So we can remove a node with a required symbol if another node with that symbol remains. That is the typical approach. The phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be a summary of the condition that all required symbols must remain. But it's ambiguous. Given the fixtures, n4 has required symbol s1 and is removable. It is the only node with symbol s1 at depth 2? There are others: n1 has s1, depth 1. So there are two nodes with s1: n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable). So we could remove n4 and still have s1 from n1. So the algorithm should allow that. Therefore, I'll interpret the rule as: we must check that after removal, all required symbols are still represented. We do not have an absolute ban on removing nodes with required symbols. So we will implement as described.\n\nAlso note: The rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" is important: a node with children that are still retained cannot be removed. Since we process deeper nodes first, we will try to remove children first, then parent may become removable if its children were removed.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). case is a dict with \"payload\": {\"nodes\": [...], \"required_symbols\": [...]}. Return a dict.\n\nWe need to write Python code that does this. Use standard library only. No file, network, etc.\n\nWe must output the source as a JSON string inside the \"implementation\" key.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nDetails:\n- Build a dictionary of nodes by id.\n- Build parent-child relationships. Also compute depth (given).\n- Initialize set retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Build a set of required symbols (list).\n- Define function to get all descendant ids of a node (including itself). We'll need to compute subtree for tentative removal. We can precompute subtree ids for each node by traversing tree. Or compute on the fly using recursion/stack. Since number of nodes is small (7 in fixtures), we can do BFS/DFS.\n\n- Sort removable nodes: first by depth descending, then by id string. Use key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']). Note: depth is integer; use negative for descending.\n\n- Iterate through sorted list:\n  if node id not in retained_ids: continue\n  if any child of node is in retained_ids: continue (since children retained)\n  Compute subtree_ids (including node) using BFS from node through children that are currently retained? But careful: if we are going to remove, we remove all descendants regardless of whether they are retained? They are all currently retained (since we haven't removed them). So we can precompute the full subtree for each node (based on original tree, not considering removals). Because if you remove a node, you remove all its descendants, even if some have already been removed? But in our iterative removal, if we remove a node, we will mark all its descendants as removed. However, if we process deeper nodes first, some descendants may have already been removed. But those descendants are no longer in retained_ids. So if a descendant has been removed already, the parent's subtree includes those ids but they are not in retained_ids. When checking if parent can be removed, we need to consider only the currently retained nodes. So we need to compute the retained subtree: nodes that are descendants of this node and are still in retained_ids. Then we consider removing those. That is more accurate. So we define a function to get all retained descendants (including node itself) by traversing children recursively, skipping nodes not in retained_ids. Then we check if removing that set would leave all required symbols.\n\nAlternatively, we can precompute full subtree, then when checking removal, we check that the set of symbols from nodes in that subtree are not the only providers of any required symbol. That is equivalent to: for each required symbol, there exists a retained node outside the subtree that provides that symbol. But if some nodes in subtree have already been removed (by previous parent removals), they are not in retained_ids anyway, so they don't contribute to the symbol set. So we need to compute the current set of symbols in the subtree that are still retained. That is what we remove.\n\nSimplify: For each candidate node n:\n - Get all nodes in its subtree that are currently retained (including n if retained). Let subtree_retained = set of ids reachable from n via children that are retained.\n - If subtree_retained is empty (node already removed?), skip.\n - Let resulting_symbols = set of symbols from all retained_ids minus subtree_retained.\n - Check if required_symbols subset of resulting_symbols.\n - Also need to check that node has no retained children? That is already covered because if it has retained children, those children would be in subtree_retained, but the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" is independent of symbol check. It says we cannot remove a node if it has retained children. So we must explicitly check: if any child of n is in retained_ids, skip. (Even if those children are also in subtree_retained, they are retained children, so we skip). So check that first.\n\n - If both conditions true, then remove: for each id in subtree_retained, remove from retained_ids. Append n['id'] to removal_order.\n\n - Continue.\n\nAfter loop, collect sorted retained_ids, and return dict.\n\nEdge case: root is not removable, but its symbol is \"root\" and required. So fine.\n\nNow produce the JSON.\n\nNote: The source code must be a valid Python string that defines solve(case). We'll use triple quotes? But inside JSON string, we need to escape. We'll write the code with proper indentation, then escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ... \\n\"}\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes and quotes.\n\nWe'll test mentally with one case.\n\nCase a-000:\nnodes: root,depth0,removable=false, utility etc.\nn1 (s1, depth1, removable=false)\nn2 (s2, depth1, removable=true)\nn3 (s0, depth1, removable=false)\nn4 (s1, depth2, parent=n2, removable=true)\nn5 (s2, depth2, parent=n3, removable=false)\nn6 (s0, depth2, parent=n1, removable=true)\n\nRequired: root, s1.\n\nRemovable nodes: n2 (depth1, id='n2'), n4 (depth2), n6 (depth2). Sort by depth desc: depth2: n4, n6 (tie by id: 'n4' < 'n6' so n4 then n6), then depth1: n2.\n\nProcess n4: root? children of n4? none (leaf). Retained children? none. Subtree retained: {n4}. Required symbols after removal: remaining retained nodes: all except n4. Symbols: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2), n6(s0). Required s1 still present (n1). So remove n4. retained_ids remove n4. removal_order = ['n4'].\n\nProcess n6: leaf, retained children none. Subtree retained: {n6}. Required symbols after removal: remove n6, remaining: root,n1,n2,n3,n5. Symbols: root,s1,s2,s0,s2. s1 still present (n1). So remove n6. retained_ids remove n6. removal_order = ['n4','n6'].\n\nProcess n2: node n2 has children: n4 (but n4 removed, so not retained). So it has no retained children. Subtree retained? n2 itself is retained, and its descendants? n4 is not retained. So subtree_retained = {n2} only. Remove n2: remaining retained_ids: root,n1,n3,n5. Symbols: root,s1,s0,s2. Required s1 still there (n1). So remove n2. removal_order = ['n4','n6','n2'].\n\nNow retained: root,n1,n3,n5. Sort ids: n1, n3, n5, root (alphabetically: n1 < n3 < n5 < root? 'root' vs 'n1': 'r' > 'n'? Actually 'n' < 'r', so n1, n3, n5, root). So retained = ['n1','n3','n5','root'].\n\nReturn {\"retained\": [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"], \"removal_order\": [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"]}.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nNow implement.\n\nWe need to be careful about child retention: after removal of n4, n2's child is gone. So we can remove n2. Good.\n\nNow code.\n\nWe'll write solve:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children lists\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # Initially all retained\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removal_order = []\n    # Get removable nodes\n    removable = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != 'root']  # root not removable anyway\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id\n    removable.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    # Function to get retained descendants (including node) under a given node\n    def retained_descendants(node_id):\n        desc = set()\n        stack = [node_id]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur not in retained:\n                continue\n            desc.add(cur)\n            for child in children[cur]:\n                stack.append(child)\n        return desc\n    # Process\n    for node in removable:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child is retained\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        subtree = retained_descendants(nid)\n        if not subtree:\n            continue\n        # Compute symbols after removal\n        symbols_after = set()\n        for rid in retained:\n            if rid not in subtree:\n                symbols_after.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\n            # Remove\n            for rid in subtree:\n                retained.discard(rid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    # Sort retained IDs\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\n    return {'retained': sorted_retained, 'removal_order': removal_order}\n\nNote: We need to ensure that root is always retained. Root has removable=false, so it won't be in removable list anyway.\n\nPotential issue: The sort by depth descending works, but what about nodes at same depth? Already tie-breaking by id string.\n\nNow produce the JSON. We'll need to escape newlines and quotes. We'll write the code as a single string.\n\nLet's produce final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1734,"completion_tokens":4739,"total_tokens":6473,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4298},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":70},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}